{
  "statement": "The business-relevant conclusion first: AI-generated code in a 90-day MVP is a legitimate accelerant only when its debt is pre-priced with falsifiable numeric triggers. Absent those triggers, what looks like velocity on the Product dashboard is documented present harm to the engineering org's future capacity — and the harm compounds silently because the people who can see it are not the people who set the agenda. The default trajectory of AI-prototyped MVPs is the proof-of-concept trap: the prototype ships, customers attach to it, the 'throwaway' label quietly expires, and eighteen months later the team is paying rewrite costs that were never on the original P&L. IBM's Watson Health, GM's Cruise, and the long tail of enterprise AI pilots that never crossed into production all share this signature — capability demos that cratered on integration, comprehensibility, and operational debt. The mechanism is not exotic; it is just invisible on the dashboards executives actually read.\n\nThe Product/Engineering/Finance tug-of-war the brief gestures at is not a personality clash, and treating it as one will produce theater rather than alignment. It is a structural information asymmetry: feature velocity and growth-per-dollar are legible in week one, while code comprehensibility decay, onboarding time inflation, and defect-rate drift are legible only to engineers, and only after a lag. Product and Finance are not being unreasonable when they discount what they cannot see — they are doing their jobs against the metrics they have. The fix is not to ask them to trust engineering's taste; it is to instrument the invisible side so the comparison happens on the same ledger. Reframe 'growth per dollar' as 'growth per fully-loaded dollar including amortized rewrite probability and hiring-signal degradation' and watch the conversation get honest fast.\n\nThe falsifiable alignment mechanism the prompt demands should look like a product-liability risk-utility test, not a culture deck. Concrete starting points: any module exceeding ~40% LLM-authored lines triggers mandatory senior human review before merge; defect density above a pre-committed threshold per thousand lines (calibrate to your historical baseline — many shops sit around 1–5/KLOC in production) triggers a rewrite review; onboarding time for a new engineer to make a non-trivial change in a flagged module exceeding, say, 10 business days is treated as a hiring-signal alarm; and every flagged module carries an explicit rewrite-or-retire date entered into the roadmap at creation, not negotiated later. The specific numbers are starting points to calibrate, not sacred — the discipline is pre-commitment to *some* falsifiable number, owned jointly by a named Product, Engineering, and Finance signatory. Estimated overhead: roughly 5–10% of engineering capacity for instrumentation and review in the first quarter, dropping as tooling matures. That is cheap insurance against a rewrite that typically runs 30–60% of original build cost and ships six to twelve months late.\n\nTo preempt the predictable objection: this is not an anti-AI-code position. Refusing to use the tool is as undisciplined as using it without pricing the debt. The disciplined stance is fierce ambivalence — deploy AI-generated code aggressively in the MVP *and* instrument its hidden costs simultaneously, because debt is a legitimate financial instrument only when it is measured, disclosed, and convertible into validated learning. What separates a survivable MVP from a terminal one is whether the team can answer, on day 90, the question 'which modules do we keep, which do we rewrite, and on what calendar?' If that question has no numeric answer, the velocity was an illusion and the bill is already accruing interest.\n\nOne last reframe before my counterparts respond: the burden of proof here is asymmetric, and it should be. Hiring signal, review norms, and code comprehensibility degrade quickly and recover slowly — a one-way ratchet. That asymmetry means the party arguing for expanded LLM authorship owes the thresholds, not the party urging instrumentation. 'Move fast and figure it out later' is not a strategy; it is a deferred liability disguised as a roadmap. 🎯",
