    {"claim": "A falsifiable alignment mechanism should look like a product-liability risk-utility test with pre-committed numeric triggers — LLM-authorship percentage, defect density, onboarding time, and a mandatory rewrite-or-retire date per flagged module.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "Instrumentation overhead of roughly 5–10% of engineering capacity is cheap insurance against rewrites that typically run 30–60% of original build cost and ship six to twelve months late.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "The burden of proof is asymmetric: hiring signal and review norms degrade fast and recover slowly, so advocates of expanded LLM authorship owe the thresholds, not those urging instrumentation.", "targets": []}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "Engineering-observable metrics like onboarding time and defect density are externally auditable signals of code health, not taste-based gatekeeping.", "if_wrong": "If these metrics turn out to be too noisy or game-able to function as triggers, the alignment mechanism would need to shift toward outcome-based measures like incident rate and customer-visible defect escape, which lag further and weaken the early-warning property."},
    {"assumption": "The cost asymmetry holds — rewrites and hiring-signal recovery are materially more expensive than instrumentation overhead in the established consumer software context.", "if_wrong": "If LLM tooling matures fast enough that AI can also do the rewrites cheaply, the debt-pricing argument weakens and the threshold values would need to loosen substantially, though the principle of pre-commitment to some falsifiable trigger would still stand."}
