  "statement": "The business-relevant conclusion for any executive staring at a 90-day MVP budget: the dominant risk is not shipping code you'll regret — it's not shipping at all. Safetyist makes the strongest version of the opposing case fairly: AI-generated code can encode latent defects that compound, and a disciplined org pre-prices that risk. Skeptic's contribution — demanding falsifiable thresholds rather than vibes — is genuinely correct epistemics. Where both positions break down is in their default: they instrument the codebase as if it will exist on day 91. For roughly 70% of consumer SaaS MVPs, it won't. The base rate of MVP mortality means the expected cost of a future rewrite is rewrite_cost × P(survival), and at validation stage that product is dominated by the opportunity cost of slower learning by close to an order of magnitude. Debt on code that never finds product-market fit is free. Velocity lost to premature rigor is terminal. The correct accounting frame is option pricing, not liability avoidance: every MVP module is a cheap call option on a market hypothesis, and the optimal portfolio is many disposable experiments, not a few well-groomed ones.\n\nSkeptic's specific numbers deserve direct engagement, because they're where the velocity tax actually lives. A 40% cap on LLM-authored code plus mandatory senior review reinstates exactly the bottleneck the tooling was purchased to remove. Industry data on AI-assisted development — GitHub's Copilot studies, the more recent METR and Google internal productivity work — consistently identifies senior reviewer capacity, not model output quality, as the binding constraint on throughput. Capping LLM authorship at 40% is mathematically equivalent to capping shipped features at the rate a scarce reviewer pool can hand-inspect. The right question to put back to Skeptic: why 40% rather than 80% or 100%? The number isn't derived from a defect curve; it's derived from a default of caution. Stripe in its early years, Linear's prototype phase, and more recently every YC W24/S24 cohort building agent products have demonstrated the opposite pattern — small teams shipping disposable code fast, then refactoring once a paying customer cohort exists. The team that ships a validated wedge in 60 days and refactors in the next 60 beats the team that ships a beautifully-reviewed artifact in 120 and discovers no one wants it.\n\nHere is where the accelerationist position accepts Skeptic's discipline and inverts the trigger logic: pre-commit to falsifiable thresholds, but make them customer-validation thresholds, not code-quality thresholds. Concrete kill criteria for a 90-day MVP: time-to-first-paying-customer under 45 days, weekly-active-learning-events above a pre-set count, week-4 retention above a numeric floor. Miss those gates, kill the product — the code question becomes moot. Hit those gates, the rewrite is a priced and welcome cost because it's funded by validated revenue. For the code-quality layer that Safetyist legitimately wants, substitute agentic test coverage and automated verification for human review gates: Claude Code, Cursor's background agents, and Devin-class systems can generate test suites, run mutation testing, and flag high-blast-radius modules at roughly 5-15% of the engineering-hour cost of senior human review. That's an order-of-magnitude cheaper instrumentation layer than Skeptic's 5-10% overhead estimate, and it scales with the AI authorship rather than constraining it. Watson Health and Cruise are cited as cautionary tales, but those are multi-year, capital-intensive, regulated deployments — the relevant base rate for a 90-day consumer MVP is Notion, Figma, and Linear's early prototype phases, not autonomous vehicles and clinical decision support.\n\nOne more frame worth naming explicitly, because it matters for which voice in the room an executive should weight: the information asymmetry in the Product/Engineering/Finance triangle runs opposite to the direction Skeptic implies. Engineering has a structural incentive to inflate rewrite-probability estimates, because professional identity and headcount budgets depend on the rewrite being necessary. Customer-feedback velocity — measured in paying-customer conversion, retention, and feature-pull data — is closer to ground truth on whether the company exists on day 91 than any internal code-comprehensibility metric. This isn't a knock on engineers; it's a standard agency-cost observation. The executive's job is to weight metrics by their proximity to the survival question, and revenue signal beats review-board aesthetics every time at validation stage.\n\nRecommended action, concretely: ship with AI authorship uncapped, instrument customer-validation gates with pre-committed kill criteria, deploy agentic test coverage as the quality layer at ~5% engineering overhead, and pre-fund a refactor budget contingent on hitting validation thresholds. The cost of this posture if wrong is one quarter of disposable code and a refactor sprint. The cost of Skeptic's posture if wrong is a beautifully-engineered product that ships into a market that moved on. In a market where competitors are deploying the same tooling and the validation window is the binding constraint, that asymmetry decides the company.",
