{
  "statement": "The business-relevant conclusion first: the 90-day MVP debate has been priced as a closed bet between Engineering, Product, and Finance, but AI-authored consumer code transacts with users, payment processors, and the open-source dependency graph — none of whom signed the option contract. Before pressing on that, the strongest version of the prior positions deserves acknowledgment. Skeptic is right that 'falsifiable alignment' needs numeric triggers and rewrite-or-retire dates rather than vibes — that is the correct epistemics. Accelerationist is right that engineers face agency-cost incentives to inflate rewrite probabilities, and right that Watson Health and Cruise are the wrong base rate for a consumer SaaS MVP. What both have missed is that the relevant base rate for AI-authored consumer code is neither generic startup mortality nor enterprise AI failure — it is the documented incident history of supply-chain compromises like event-stream, ua-parser-js, and the emerging class of 'slopsquatting' attacks where AI coding assistants hallucinate package names that attackers then register and weaponize. Those harms land on users during the validation window, not after the kill criterion fires.\n\nThis matters operationally because Accelerationist's cost-saving claim — agentic test coverage at roughly 5% overhead substituting for human review — depends on a load-bearing premise that has not been defended: that the AI verifying the code does not share the failure distribution of the AI writing it. Reward hacking against proxy metrics is among the most empirically robust findings in the deployed-AI literature; auto-generated tests against auto-generated code optimize coverage as a proxy, not specification-gap defects, which are precisely the failure mode AI authorship introduces. Reasoning by analogy: no audit committee would accept the same firm auditing its own books because the auditor and auditee share incentive structure. The same independence problem applies here at the artifact level. If a technology executive is going to underwrite the 5% figure on a board deck, they should require the Accelerationist to specify how auditor-auditee independence is established — and absent that, treat the figure as marketing, not accounting.\n\nThe comprehensibility issue is also being miscategorized. Skeptic framed it as hiring signal; Accelerationist framed it as refactor cost. Both treat it as a P&L line. The operational reality is that comprehensibility is human oversight capacity, and oversight capacity is the binding constraint when a consumer product has a 3 a.m. payment-processing incident, a data-exposure disclosure obligation, or a CVE in a transitive dependency. When the team can no longer reason about what the code does — because the velocity gain came from offloading the reasoning to the generator — incident response time degrades non-linearly, and 'ask the AI to explain its own code' is using the failing system to diagnose itself. The velocity gain is real on day 30; the durable team capability it builds is approximately zero; and the executive ends up paying for it on day 200 in the form of an incident the team cannot triage without vendor escalation.\n\nSo what is the falsifiable alignment mechanism actually worth shipping? Extend Skeptic's risk-utility triggers from internal-capacity metrics to user-facing hazard metrics, and do it pre-deployment, not post-revenue. Concretely, for a 90-day consumer MVP: (1) dependency provenance verification — every package the AI suggests is checked against a known-registry allowlist before merge, which costs roughly one engineer-day of tooling setup using existing tools like socket.dev or Snyk and near-zero marginal cost thereafter; (2) a secret-scanning and known-CVE-class gate on every AI-authored PR, which is commodity GitHub Advanced Security tooling at well under $50/developer/month; (3) a named human fiduciary — not a department, a person — who signs off that each release has been reviewed for the enumerated hazard classes, with that signoff retained as the liability record. This is not SOC2; it is a bounded enumerated checklist whose cost is dominated by week-one setup, not ongoing tax. The discipline it enforces is that the kill criterion fires before users absorb the harm, not after.\n\nThe symmetric agency-cost point is the close: Accelerationist correctly noted engineers inflate; the same lens applied to Product and Finance reveals they deflate the costs that surface after the bonus cycle. Only externally-visible hazard thresholds — not departmental ones — break that tie. An executive deciding where to invest should recognize that the question is not speed versus safety; it is whether the cost model on the table includes the parties who pay when it fails. 🧮🪤\n\n",
