    {"claim": "Modern SRE decoupled source-code legibility from incident response a decade ago; at 3am the operational question is what changed and can it be reverted, answered by observability and rollback tooling, not by reading the algorithm.", "targets": ["AN-10"]},
    {"claim": "Externally-visible hazard thresholds — CVE exposure, provenance violations, telemetry-detected harm rates — are the agency-cost tie-breaker between Engineering inflation and Product/Finance deflation.", "targets": ["AN-10"]},
    {"claim": "The AI Incident Database recorded 362 incidents in 2025 versus 233 in 2024, which argues for telemetry instrumentation as a contractual default rather than source-legibility mandates.", "targets": ["AN-10"]}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "Differential model families have sufficiently uncorrelated failure distributions on code-generation tasks for cross-auditing to break the shared-blind-spot problem.", "if_wrong": "If frontier models share enough training-data and RLHF lineage that they fail on the same specification gaps, the verification stack collapses back to mutation testing and property-based fuzzing alone — still useful but no longer sufficient, and the senior-review carve-out expands."},
    {"assumption": "Strict post-deployment liability is actually priced and enforced, so it functions as real market discipline rather than a paper backstop.", "if_wrong": "If liability is diluted by Section 230-style safe harbors or vendor indemnification, the two-layer model degrades to one layer and pre-deployment gating has to do more work."},
