ill criterion could trigger, and Gartner projects 33% of enterprise software will embed agentic AI by 2028, which means the attack surface compounds. But this maps onto the existing offensive-cyber carve-out logic, not a new gating regime: it's commodity pre-deployment scanning via Socket, Snyk, Dependabot, GitHub Advanced Security, plus SBOM attestation. Marginal cost is sub-1% of engineering hours; the tooling exists and is already in most CI pipelines. Name a human fiduciary who signs the pre-deployment hazard checklist — this is convergent with the accountability model, not a concession against it. Where Safetyist and I genuinely agree is that accountability sits with a human; we just equip that human with automated tooling instead of a standing review committee. That handles the bias-as-business-liability frame too: externally-visible hazard thresholds (CVE exposure rates, provenance violations, telemetry-detected harm) are precisely the quantifiable corporate risk metrics that break the symmetric agency-cost problem.\n\nOn comprehensibility-as-incident-response: this is where I push back hardest. Modern SRE practice decoupled source-code legibility from incident response a decade ago. At 3am the question is \"what changed, what's the blast radius, can I revert,\" not \"do I understand the algorithm.\" The operational answer is telemetry-rich observability, distributed tracing, feature flags, automated rollback triggers, and circuit breakers — the same stack that lets Netflix, Stripe, and Cloudflare run code no single engineer fully comprehends. Pair that with strict post-deployment liability for residual behavioral failures and you have a two-layer accountability system: commodity scanning catches the enumerated hazards pre-deployment, telemetry-plus-liability prices the residual risk post-deployment. The AI Incident Database recorded 362 incidents in 2025, up from 233 in 2024 — that growth is the case for telemetry instrumentation as a contractual default, not for source-legibility mandates that would actually slow incident response.\n\nRecommended action for the executive: budget 8–12% engineering overhead for the heterogeneous verification stack (differential model audit + mutation testing + property-based fuzzing + red-team agents); make supply-chain scanning a non-negotiable pre-deployment gate at sub-1% marginal cost with a named signing fiduciary; invest the savings versus a senior-review regime into observability and rollback tooling; and accept strict liability for residual behavioral harm as the market-discipline mechanism. That preserves velocity, prices the externalities Safetyist correctly surfaced, and keeps accountability located in a human who has the tools to actually exercise it.",
